"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,529\nThe Learning Brain. Series 5: Belonging\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,365\nEpisode 7\n\n3\n00:00:07,445 --> 00:00:09,597\nThe teacher\u2019s mindset\n\n4\n00:00:12,397 --> 00:00:15,750\nSome instructors believe students either have what it takes,\n\n5\n00:00:15,909 --> 00:00:16,818\nor they don't.\n\n6\n00:00:17,384 --> 00:00:20,867\nOthers believe ability grows with effort and good teaching.\n\n7\n00:00:21,311 --> 00:00:23,982\nDoes that belief show up in students' grades?\n\n8\n00:00:24,152 --> 00:00:26,203\nAt a large public university,\n\n9\n00:00:26,476 --> 00:00:31,801\nresearchers surveyed a hundred and fifty STEM faculty about their beliefs on ability.\n\n10\n00:00:32,323 --> 00:00:38,002\nThen they examined more than fifteen thousand student records from those faculty members' courses,\n\n11\n00:00:38,245 --> 00:00:39,673\nacross two years.\n\n12\n00:00:39,843 --> 00:00:43,335\nIn courses taught by faculty with more fixed mindsets,\n\n13\n00:00:43,629 --> 00:00:50,545\nthe racial achievement gap was about twice as large as in courses taught by faculty with growth mindsets.\n\n14\n00:00:51,059 --> 00:00:54,932\nAll students tended to do worse in the fixed mindset courses,\n\n15\n00:00:55,238 --> 00:00:58,538\nbut students from under represented groups were hit hardest.\n\n16\n00:00:58,708 --> 00:01:02,746\nThe instructor's mindset predicted these gaps better than their gender,\n\n17\n00:01:02,974 --> 00:01:06,110\nrace, age, teaching experience,\n\n18\n00:01:06,130 --> 00:01:06,959\nor tenure.\n\n19\n00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:11,826\nAnd students in fixed mindset courses reported feeling less motivated,\n\n20\n00:01:12,170 --> 00:01:15,329\neven though they did not rate those courses as harder.\n\n21\n00:01:15,499 --> 00:01:17,731\nHow might a belief leak into a course?\n\n22\n00:01:18,047 --> 00:01:21,516\nThrough comments like, not everyone is cut out for this.\n\n23\n00:01:21,847 --> 00:01:25,466\nThrough advising students to drop after one bad exam.\n\n24\n00:01:25,768 --> 00:01:28,539\nThrough praising only speed and brilliance.\n\n25\n00:01:28,832 --> 00:01:31,867\nAnd through grading that offers no second chance.\n\n26\n00:01:32,037 --> 00:01:33,001\nSome caution.\n\n27\n00:01:33,432 --> 00:01:36,617\nThis is a correlational study at one university,\n\n28\n00:01:36,834 --> 00:01:40,506\nand faculty with fixed mindsets may differ in other ways.\n\n29\n00:01:40,870 --> 00:01:43,652\nBut the signals you send are under your control.\n\n30\n00:01:43,822 --> 00:01:44,572\nFor teaching.\n\n31\n00:01:44,915 --> 00:01:47,504\nTalk about ability as something students develop.\n\n32\n00:01:47,949 --> 00:01:49,866\nPraise strategies and improvement,\n\n33\n00:01:49,886 --> 00:01:50,978\nnot just talent.\n\n34\n00:01:51,474 --> 00:01:54,136\nDon't suggest dropping after a single setback.\n\n35\n00:01:54,507 --> 00:01:56,487\nAnd give chances to show growth,\n\n36\n00:01:56,572 --> 00:01:58,567\nlike revisions or retakes.\n\n37\n00:01:58,991 --> 00:02:04,887\nOne tip to share with students: struggling in a hard course is not proof that you lack ability.\n\n38\n00:02:05,057 --> 00:02:06,666\nWhat you believe about ability,\n\n39\n00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:08,277\nyour students can hear.\n\n"
